<!--
  The two ways of looking at the Colors.

  Structure mirrored from Ideology/Defs/MemeDefs/Memes_GenderSupremacy.xml, which
  is vanilla's working implementation of "one group is innately superior, with
  mechanical consequences". The Color hierarchy is the same shape, so it uses the
  same machinery: a MemeGroupDef, an abstract base carrying the exclusion tag, and
  two mutually exclusive concrete memes.

  These are opposed by construction. An ideoligion cannot hold both, which is what
  makes the Society and the resistance legible as opposites rather than as two
  flavours of the same thing.

  WHAT CARRIES THE HIERARCHY MECHANICALLY

  Not these memes on their own. Biotech already ships the machinery in
  Biotech/Defs/PreceptDefs/Precepts_Xenotype.xml: the PreferredXenotype precept
  (preceptClass Precept_Xenotype) gives +10 opinion toward a preferred xenotype,
  -30 toward a disliked one, a colony-makeup mood effect, and a SelfDislikedXenotype
  thought for a pawn who IS the disliked kind. That last one is the caste system
  landing on the person living inside it, and vanilla wrote it for us.

  It is a TWO-TIER mechanic though - preferred versus everything else, capped at
  three preferred xenotypes. A graded fifteen-rung hierarchy where highColors
  outrank midColors outrank lowColors would need a custom ThoughtWorker in C#.
  See docs/PROGRESS.md.
-->
<Defs>

  <MemeGroupDef>
    <defName>RR_ColorOrder</defName>
    <drawOffset>(0.65, 0.23)</drawOffset>
    <maxRows>1</maxRows>
    <renderOrder>2100</renderOrder>
  </MemeGroupDef>

  <MemeDef Name="RR_ColorOrderBase" Abstract="True">
    <groupDef>RR_ColorOrder</groupDef>
    <exclusionTags>
      <li>RR_ColorOrder</li>
    </exclusionTags>
  </MemeDef>

  <!-- ============================ the Society ============================= -->

  <MemeDef ParentName="RR_ColorOrderBase">
    <defName>RR_ColorHierarchy</defName>
    <label>Color hierarchy</label>
    <description>The Colors are not a policy. They are a fact of biology, arrived at by better minds than ours, and a person's worth is settled at conception. Golds rule because Golds are built to rule. Reds dig because Reds are built to dig. To resent the arrangement is to resent arithmetic.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Memes/RR_ColorHierarchy</iconPath>
    <impact>3</impact>
    <renderOrder>90</renderOrder>
    <!--
      Razors and pulse weapons are the tools of the ruling caste, and carrying one
      is a statement about where you stand. Neolithic weapons are what the tribes
      were left with after the revolt failed, and picking one up says the same
      thing in reverse.
    -->
    <preferredWeaponClasses>
      <noble>Ultratech</noble>
      <despised>Neolithic</despised>
    </preferredWeaponClasses>
    <generalRules>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>memeAdjective->hierarchical</li>
        <li>memeAdjective->ordered</li>
        <li>memeAdjective->aureate</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->auri</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->chroma</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the hierarchy</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the Colors</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the pyramid</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->sovereign</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->archgovernor</li>
        <li>memeMoralist->quality controller</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </generalRules>
    <descriptionMaker>
      <rules>
        <rulesStrings>
          <li>creation(tag=meme_RR_ColorHierarchy)       ->[deity0_name] set each kind of person to the task they were shaped for, and called the arrangement good.</li>
          <li>episode(uses=1,tag=meme_RR_ColorHierarchy) ->[deity0_name] said: a hand is not lesser than an eye for being a hand, and neither may take the other's place.</li>
          <li>setup(tag=meme_RR_ColorHierarchy)          ->[founderName] held that a society of equals is a society of no one doing anything well, and set about sorting people into what they were for.</li>
          <li>archistFact(tag=meme_RR_ColorHierarchy)    ->The great machines sorted humanity once, correctly, and it is not our place to re-sort it.</li>
        </rulesStrings>
      </rules>
    </descriptionMaker>
    <!--
      GUARANTEES THE PRECEPTS ARE ACTUALLY TAKEN, and leaving it out is why the
      razor lock and the Color ladder both did nothing in play.

      associatedMemes on a PreceptDef only makes it ELIGIBLE for an ideoligion
      carrying that meme. It does not make generation pick it, and with no "none"
      option on the issue an ideoligion could carry RR_ColorHierarchy and simply
      never take a stance on razors or deference at all. The issue still appears
      in the ideoligion UI - every issue does - which makes the omission look like
      it worked.

      requireOne is the vanilla lever for this: a list of lists, each inner list a
      set from which the ideoligion MUST take exactly one. Mirrored from
      Ideology/Defs/MemeDefs/Memes_GroupRelation.xml, which uses it to force a
      slavery stance and an execution stance.
    -->
    <requireOne>
      <li>
        <li>RR_RazorBearing_GoldOnly</li>
      </li>
      <!--
        WHO WEARS THE PLATE. Required rather than merely associated, for the same
        reason as everything else in this list: associatedMemes only makes a
        precept ELIGIBLE, and an ideoligion that carried the meme without taking a
        stance on the issue would leave the armour restriction in
        Source/RimRising/CasteArmor.cs enforcing nothing at all, while the issue
        still showed up in the ideoligion UI looking as though it worked.
      -->
      <li>
        <li>RR_ArmorBearing_Restricted</li>
      </li>
      <!--
        SCARS. Vanilla Ideology already models this completely - the Scarification
        issue, its tiers, and the ScarificationCeremony ritual - and none of those
        precepts claim a meme of their own, so they are free to be required here.
        Nothing needed building.

        Tiered by who holds the meme. The hierarchy proper marks deliberately and
        once: the Peerless Scar is a single line from ear to jaw and it means a
        specific thing, so Minor or Heavy. Gold worship as the tribes practise it
        is not restrained about it, so Heavy or Extreme.
      -->
      <li>
        <li>RR_Scarification_Peerless</li>
        <li>Scarification_Heavy</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_ColorDeference_Graded</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_InterColorUnion_Defect</li>
        <li>RR_InterColorUnion_Unbecoming</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_Carving_Abomination</li>
      </li>
    </requireOne>
  </MemeDef>

  <!-- ========================== the obsidian tribes ======================= -->

  <!--
    Distinct from RR_ColorHierarchy on purpose, and the distinction matters
    mechanically as well as in the fiction.

    The Society's hierarchy is an administrative doctrine: a Compact, a Board of
    Quality Control, courts, and an order of knights to enforce it. What was done
    to the Obsidians after the Dark Revolt was not that. They were moved to the
    poles, stripped of every tool worth the name, and taught by bribed shamans
    that the Colors above them are literally divine. They obey gods, not statutes.

    Giving the tribes RR_ColorHierarchy handed them Olympic Knights, because the
    knighthoods are gated on that meme. They are a Gold order serving the Compact,
    and the tribes have no Compact and no Golds. This meme keeps the tribes
    subordinate to Gold without making them administrators of a system they were
    deliberately cut out of.
  -->
  <MemeDef ParentName="RR_ColorOrderBase">
    <defName>RR_GoldWorship</defName>
    <label>the sky gods</label>
    <description>The gold ones come down out of the sky, and what they take they were owed, and what they leave is mercy. This has been true since before the ice, and the shamans have the stories to prove it.\n\nNobody now living remembers being told it for the first time.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Memes/RR_GoldWorship</iconPath>
    <impact>3</impact>
    <renderOrder>92</renderOrder>
    <generalRules>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>memeAdjective->godbound</li>
        <li>memeAdjective->skyward</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->sky</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->god</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the sky gods</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the descent</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->queen</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->jarl</li>
        <li>memeMoralist->shaman</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </generalRules>
    <descriptionMaker>
      <rules>
        <rulesStrings>
          <li>creation(tag=meme_RR_GoldWorship)       ->[deity0_name] came down burning out of the black, and the ice has belonged to them ever since.</li>
          <li>episode(uses=1,tag=meme_RR_GoldWorship) ->[deity0_name] walked among the tents once, and those who touched them were never seen again, which is how we know it was true.</li>
          <li>setup(tag=meme_RR_GoldWorship)          ->[founderName] was the first shaman to hear the sky speak, and carried down the terms.</li>
        </rulesStrings>
      </rules>
    </descriptionMaker>
    <!--
      GUARANTEES THE PRECEPTS ARE ACTUALLY TAKEN, and leaving it out is why the
      razor lock and the Color ladder both did nothing in play.

      associatedMemes on a PreceptDef only makes it ELIGIBLE for an ideoligion
      carrying that meme. It does not make generation pick it, and with no "none"
      option on the issue an ideoligion could carry RR_ColorHierarchy and simply
      never take a stance on razors or deference at all. The issue still appears
      in the ideoligion UI - every issue does - which makes the omission look like
      it worked.

      requireOne is the vanilla lever for this: a list of lists, each inner list a
      set from which the ideoligion MUST take exactly one. Mirrored from
      Ideology/Defs/MemeDefs/Memes_GroupRelation.xml, which uses it to force a
      slavery stance and an execution stance.
    -->
    <requireOne>
      <li>
        <li>RR_ColorDeference_Graded</li>
      </li>
      <!-- The tribes cut deeper, and for different reasons. See above. -->
      <li>
        <li>Scarification_Heavy</li>
        <li>Scarification_Extreme</li>
        <li>RR_Scarification_Peerless</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_Carving_Abomination</li>
      </li>
    </requireOne>
  </MemeDef>

  <!-- =========================== the resistance =========================== -->

  <MemeDef ParentName="RR_ColorOrderBase">
    <defName>RR_ColorEquality</defName>
    <label>Color equality</label>
    <description>Somebody decided which of us would be strong and which of us would be short-lived, and then called the result nature. It was not nature. It was a decision, made by people, for their own convenience, and a decision can be unmade.\n\nWhat a person was built for is not what a person is for.</description>
    <iconPath>UI/Memes/RR_ColorEquality</iconPath>
    <impact>3</impact>
    <renderOrder>91</renderOrder>
    <generalRules>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>memeAdjective->levelling</li>
        <li>memeAdjective->unsorted</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->free</li>
        <li>memeHyphenPrefix->iso</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the rising</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the unmaking</li>
        <li>memeConcept->the reap</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->voice</li>
        <li>memeLeaderNoun->reaper</li>
        <li>memeMoralist->levelspeaker</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </generalRules>
    <descriptionMaker>
      <rules>
        <rulesStrings>
          <li>creation(tag=meme_RR_ColorEquality)       ->[deity0_name] made one kind of person, and everything that came after was done by hands, in workshops, for money.</li>
          <li>episode(uses=1,tag=meme_RR_ColorEquality) ->[deity0_name] asked who had given them the right to decide, and no one present could answer.</li>
          <li>setup(tag=meme_RR_ColorEquality)          ->[founderName] came up out of the mines, saw what the sorting had cost, and decided it would be undone or they would die trying.</li>
          <li>archistFact(tag=meme_RR_ColorEquality)    ->Even the great machines never presumed to rank a person by their making.</li>
        </rulesStrings>
      </rules>
    </descriptionMaker>
    <!--
      GUARANTEES THE PRECEPTS ARE ACTUALLY TAKEN, and leaving it out is why the
      razor lock and the Color ladder both did nothing in play.

      associatedMemes on a PreceptDef only makes it ELIGIBLE for an ideoligion
      carrying that meme. It does not make generation pick it, and with no "none"
      option on the issue an ideoligion could carry RR_ColorHierarchy and simply
      never take a stance on razors or deference at all. The issue still appears
      in the ideoligion UI - every issue does - which makes the omission look like
      it worked.

      requireOne is the vanilla lever for this: a list of lists, each inner list a
      set from which the ideoligion MUST take exactly one. Mirrored from
      Ideology/Defs/MemeDefs/Memes_GroupRelation.xml, which uses it to force a
      slavery stance and an execution stance.
    -->
    <requireOne>
      <li>
        <li>RR_RazorBearing_Anyone</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_ColorDeference_None</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_InterColorUnion_Free</li>
      </li>
      <li>
        <li>RR_Carving_Liberation</li>
      </li>
      <!-- The mirror of RR_ArmorBearing_Restricted, and required for the same
           reason: without it an equality ideoligion could take no stance on the
           issue at all, which reads in the UI as a stance and behaves as none. -->
      <li>
        <li>RR_ArmorBearing_Earned</li>
      </li>
    </requireOne>
  </MemeDef>

</Defs>
